    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d530">

                <pb facs="0603" n="591" sameAs="frus:frus1944v03/pg591"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 European War 1939/2465: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Finland</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gullion</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Helsinki</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-04-12T00:00:00+02:00">April 12,
                            1944—midnight</date>.<lb/>[Received April 13—8:19 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>322. Diet today rejected Soviet terms in session practically identical with that
                    of March 15.</p>
                <p>Government’s presentation of case<anchor n="13" xml:id="d530-f1"/> indicated a
                    desire to continue negotiations for more favorable terms and in proceeding to
                    order of day without a recorded vote Diet approved of Government’s action. Dr.
                    Ramsay has promised to give me text of Finnish reply when drafted.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Stockholm as our 75.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gullion</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d530-f1" n="13" xml:id="d530-f1-content">
                <p>In telegram 354, April 26, 1944, the Chargé reported part of the statement that
                    Prime Minister Linkomies had made to the Diet: “The Government has decided
                    unanimously … that it cannot accept the conditions presented.… While it is true
                    that no assurance can be offered that circumstances would be such that better
                    conditions might be obtained in the future, nevertheless we cannot be persuaded
                    by fear of unfavorable developments to agree to conditions disastrous for the
                    nation’s existence.” (740.00119 European War 1939/2529)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
